# What this is

An AI assistant for **independent pharmacies** that automates prescription
data entry and prepares a drafted record for **pharmacist verification**.
It sits on top of an existing pharmacy management system (PMS) — it is not
a PMS and never replaces one. Pilot PMS: **Liberty (RxQ)**, via its
PIN-authenticated API.

Pipeline: intake → extraction → match/validate → verification draft → write-back.

Full strategy, market position, and phase plan: `docs/game-plan.pdf`.

# Non-negotiables

- **Pharmacist-in-the-loop, always.** Never generate code that dispenses,
  finalizes, or writes back a prescription without an explicit pharmacist
  action. There is no "auto-approve" path, even for all-green records.
  All-green earns a *fast lane* — one tap to accept — never zero-touch.
- **The model never assigns clinical tiers.** It reports what it read and
  how confident it was. Tiers are computed by our scorer from multiple
  signals. Don't shortcut this.
- **Liberty's own clinical/DUR engine stays authoritative** at final
  verification. Our pre-checks surface concerns earlier; they don't replace it.
- **Everything is auditable.** Any change to a prescription field emits a
  CorrectionEvent. Audit tables are append-only — never UPDATE or DELETE.

# v1 scope decisions (don't relitigate these in code)

- **API-first, never screen-scraping.** Liberty exposes a documented,
  PIN-authenticated API. Vision-driving-the-PMS is a last-resort fallback,
  not an architecture.
- **NCPDP SCRIPT is the data model.** We ride the e-prescribing rails rather
  than inventing a parallel vocabulary. eScripts are already structured — the
  easy 60–70% of volume.
- **No standalone drug-interaction engine in v1.** NLM discontinued its free
  interaction API in Jan 2024. Building or licensing one (Medi-Span/FDB, or
  NLP over openFDA labels) is a deliberate Phase-4 build-vs-buy decision.
  Until then, Liberty's DUR is the authoritative interaction check.
- **RxNorm/openFDA live endpoints for the pilot.** Mirror to Postgres later,
  only if latency or rate limits actually bite.
- **Polling first.** TanStack Query against a Postgres-backed queue.
  Websockets only when staleness is a real, observed complaint.

# The metric that matters

Median pharmacist **seconds-per-script at verification**, measured against
Anjali's Week-1 baseline. Time-to-verify is the headline; errors caught
before the bench is the trust story. Optimize accordingly.

# Stack

TypeScript everywhere. Next.js (App Router) + Prisma + PostgreSQL + Zod.
pnpm workspaces monorepo. Prefer boring, well-tested choices over clever ones.
